    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The movement held that black liberation would come not only from political and structural change but from a psychological transformation in which black people believed in the value of their blackness.</li><li>It asked white allies to understand and support, but not to lead, and it defined black to include South Africans of Indian descent and other people of colour.</li><li>Many of its leaders were banned or imprisoned and its organisations were outlawed in 1977, a month after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> died in police custody, yet its language and strategy spread through most corners of black South African politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By 19 June 1976, three days after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> began, 123 key members of the movement had been banned and assigned to internal exile in remote rural districts.</p></aside>
    </section>

